R"VG(
# medieval9.vg (H-04; the second pass 2026-10-10): the pilgrim's guide to the people's holy place: the road from the record's place by
# its own landmarks, the nights and their keepers, the water, the dangers of the year, the customs of the road, and what to do there.

genre.pilgrim (words=40..130)
  (2) {pilgrim.open}\n{pilgrim.leg}\n{pilgrim.leg}\n{pilgrim.end}
  {pilgrim.open}\n{pilgrim.leg}\n{pilgrim.leg}\n{pilgrim.leg}\n{pilgrim.end}

pilgrim.open (formula)
  For the pilgrim to {holyplace}, from {$home}: the way, the water and the nights.
  How to go up to {holyplace} at {festival}, written for those who have not gone before.
  The road to {holyplace}, as the old pilgrims walked it, for the young ones who will.
  From {$home} to {holyplace}: what to carry, where to sleep, what to say.

pilgrim.leg
  (2 once) {med.pg.from}; {med.pg.night}.
  (2 once) {med.pg.from}; {med.pg.night}.
  (once) {med.pg.water}
  (once) {med.pg.danger}
  (once) {med.pg.custom}
  (once) {med.pg.custom}
  (once) {med.pg.sight}

med.pg.from
  From {$home} take the road toward {mountain}
  Leave {$home} by {town.edge} {time.of}
  From {$seat}, follow {river} upstream for {#n|day}
  Cross {land} by the old track, keeping {skyone} on your left
  (has:$home.landmark) Go out past {$home.landmark} and keep straight on
  Take the low road through the {crop} fields, not the high one, which is shorter and worse
  Go by {city2}, where the market is, and buy nothing there
  Follow the stones painted {colour.holy} along the road
  Walk with the drovers and their {herd} as far as the fork; they know the way and the dogs know them

med.pg.night
  the first night is at {pilgrim.stop}, where the {food} is good
  sleep at {pilgrim.stop}; the keeper takes {#n} {smallcoin} and gives a blanket
  the second night is at {pilgrim.stop}, where you will sleep badly and be glad of it
  the nights are cold there, so carry {garment}
  at {pilgrim.stop} they give {drink} to pilgrims and charge for the cup
  stop at {pilgrim.stop} and not before, whatever your feet say

pilgrim.stop
  {$seat}
  the shrine by the road
  the house of the ferryman
  the last village before the hills
  the inn with the {colour.any} door
  the old barn of {holyplace}
  {city2}

med.pg.water
  (desert=1) Carry water for {#n|day}; the cisterns on this stretch are dry in {season.now}.
  (desert=0) {river} is high in {season.now}; wait for the ferry and do not swim it.
  Drink at {watersrc} and nowhere else; the ditches are for the {herd}.
  The {watersrc.n} at the third stone is sweet; the one after it is not, whatever the boys selling cups tell you.
  There is no water between the fork and {mountain}. Fill every {vessel.one.n}, even your hat.
  The water at {pilgrim.stop} is good, but the {drink} is better and safer.
  (desert=1) At the dry {watersrc.n} by the road, do not wait for the water-seller; he does not come any more.
  (desert=0) The streams below {mountain} are cold enough to stop your heart; cup your hands and be quick.
  Carry {food} for {#n|day}; the villages on the road sell it at pilgrims' prices.

med.pg.danger (share=0.5)
  There are robbers on this road in hard years, and they take {crop2} as gladly as {coin}.
  (desert=0) There are wolves in the woods above the ford; walk in company.
  The dogs of the villages bite pilgrims, and only pilgrims. Carry a stick.
  The toll-keepers at the bridge will ask for {#n} {smallcoin}. The toll is half that.
  In {weather} the track over {mountain} is not a track. Wait.
  (war=1) The war has come near the road. Ask at every village which way the soldiers went, and go the other.
  (sick=1) There is sickness on the road this year. Sleep outside, and do not share a cup.
  The ferryman drinks. Look at him before you get in.
  The {herd} of {city2} are loose on the road at dusk; walk round them, not through.
  The inn at the fork waters its {drink}; the inn after it waters it less.
  The children who offer to carry your load will carry it to their mother's house.
  The stones on the last stretch are loose. Watch your feet, not {holyplace}.
  The holy men at the gate of {holyplace} are not holy and not men of the gate. Pay nobody until you are inside.
  Beware the {beast} at the ferryman's house; it has eaten better pilgrims than you.
  The {pest} are in every inn on this road; sleep in your {garment.n}.
  (desert=1) The sand comes up in the afternoons below {mountain}; walk early and rest under the {tree} at noon.
  (desert=0) The bridge at the fork is {walls.of} on one side and rotten timber on the other. Keep to the {walls.of}.
  The road is busy with {herd} going to market at {city2}; they have the right of way, and they know it.

med.pg.custom (share=0.5)
  At the stone with the {#number} marks, turn toward {skyone} at first light.
  Give the keeper of the ford {#n} {smallcoin}, or a song of {$home} if you have none.
  Tie a {colour.holy} rag to the {tree.n} at the crossroads, as everyone does, and do not ask why.
  Leave {offering} at the shrine by the road; it is eaten by the poor, which is the point.
  At the top of the hill, look back once at {$home}, and then not again.
  Walk the last stretch in silence, even the children; it is harder than it sounds.
  Carry a stone from {$home} and leave it at {holyplace} on the pile; everyone's stone is there.
  Wear {colour.holy} on the last day, if you have it; borrow it if you do not.
  Say your mother's name at every shrine, and your own at {holyplace}, and the {herd}'s if you like.
  At the bridge, drop a crumb of {food} in the water for whoever is under it.
  At the third shrine, take off your shoes and carry them with your {garment.n}; nobody remembers why, and everybody does it.
  Where the road crosses {river}, wash your face and hands, and say nothing until you are across; {med.pg.why}.
  At the {tree.n} with {motif.over} cut in its bark, touch it with your left hand.
  Sing the walking song of {$home} on the steep part; it was made for that hill.
  Do not count the steps to {holyplace}. Everybody does, and everybody gets a different number.
  At the half-way stone, eat {food} with whoever is there, even strangers; it is the rule of the road.
  Pick a sprig of {crop2} at the last farm and carry it in; the priests expect it.
  When you see the {colour.holy} banners, stop talking about prices.
  Burn a pinch of {fuel} at the shrine of the ford, for the ones who drowned there.
  (moons>0) If {moon} is full on the road, walk at night; the old pilgrims said it was luckier.

med.pg.why
  the river listens
  the old ones did
  the {herd} are watching
  it is the custom of {$home}

med.pg.sight (share=0.5)
  From the pass you will see {holyplace} for the first time. Many weep. You may.
  The {tree} by the ford are older than the road, they say, and the road is older than {city}.
  Here the road is steep; the old go slowly and arrive anyway.
  At the turning you will see {motif.over} cut in the rock by people older than ours.
  Below the hill the {herd} graze among the old stones, and nobody minds them.
  From the ridge you can see {sea} on a clear day, and the {roofs} of {city2} on a very clear one.
  At dusk the {bird} come down to {river} by the thousand; stop and watch, you have time.
  The road passes the old battlefield; the {crop} grows there taller than anywhere.
  You will pass {house.one} where an old woman gives {drink} to pilgrims for nothing. Thank her.
  (moons>0) If you walk at night, {moon} lights the white stones of the road. Follow them.
  On the last day you will smell {holyplace} before you see it: {fuel} smoke and {crop2}.
  Near the end the road is lined with {tree} that pilgrims planted, one each, for {#nbig} years.
  You will pass the {craft}-house where they make the {colour.holy} cloth for {holyplace}; the women will sell you a strip.
  The last village keeps {herd} for the pilgrims' milk, and charges for it the price of {drink}.

pilgrim.end
  At {holyplace}, {rite.imp}, and {med.pg.then}
  At {holyplace}, {rite.imp}, and {med.pg.then}
  At {holyplace} they keep {feast.custom}. {med.pg.keep}
  At {holyplace} the priests sell {med.pg.sold}. {med.pg.buy}
  At {holyplace}, {med.pg.at}
  At {holyplace} {med.pg.see}

med.pg.then (share=0.5)
  go home by the other road, which is longer and better.
  then eat; you have earned it, and so has your mother, who did it before you.
  then sit a while; nobody will move you on.
  go home before you start to find fault with it.
  then go and look at {mountain} from the steps; that is half of it.
  then sleep wherever they put you, and do not complain about the {beast} next to you.

med.pg.keep (share=0.5)
  Keep it with them, and then go home and tell nobody you cried.
  Keep it with them, and keep your purse in your shirt.
  Join in; nobody minds if you do it wrong.
  Watch the first year; do it the second.

med.pg.buy (share=0.4)
  Buy one if you must; {god} does not mind either way.
  Buy nothing; the same {vessel} are cheaper at {city2}.
  Buy a {colour.any} one; the {colour.holy} ones are for the priests' families.
  Buy one for your mother; she will not believe you went otherwise, and put it on the shelf with the {vessel}.

med.pg.at (share=0.5)
  say your name, and they say {god} says it back. Listen hard.
  sleep one night on the floor with the others. You will not sleep. That is the night.
  give what you brought and take nothing but the dust on your feet.
  find the oldest priest and ask for the story of the founding; the young ones tell it wrong.
  stand where the light comes in at noon. That is all; it is enough.

med.pg.see (share=0.4)
  you will see what you came to see. Do not ask the priests what it means; they will tell you.
  you will see {focusword}, if it is that time of year; if it is not, you will see the {med.chr.trades} selling {vessel}.
  you will see more people than live in {$home}, all of them praying for rain or against it.
  you will find it smaller than the songs and larger than you.

med.pg.sold
  little {vessel} of the water
  {colour.holy} ribbons
  clay {herd}
  stones that are not from the hill
  bread that is not blessed
)VG"
